{
 "id": "2026-yu-makerspace",
 "name": "Makerspace Colour Preference and Comfort",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original images: 12 rendered views of a generic makerspace interior (white curved walls with round niches, white seating) whose carpet and niche colour takes one of four hue conditions of the paper's HSL hue series, A8 (blue) and A5 (red), rated highest in the laboratory experiment, and A3 (green) and A7 (purple), rated lower; each hue is shown from three viewpoints (eye level, high-angle overview, wide-angle perspective). Participants imagine entering the space to carry out a semester-long innovation project. Each of the 12 images is shown in random order after a 2 s grey screen for 8 s, and is then rated on two 5-point scales, Preference and Comfort. Age and gender are asked at the end. Rebuilt from Yu, Li & Tang (2026), Contextual decoupling in color preference: multimodal evidence from spatial evaluation in makerspaces, Frontiers in Psychology 17: 1826920 (Experiment 3 viewpoint validation, 68 participants), with the renders cropped from its Figure 4 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: hue_view, e.g. A8_eye, A5_high, A3_wide. Analysis as in the paper: mean Preference and Comfort per hue and viewpoint, and rank-order stability against the laboratory means (Spearman's rho); the paper found A8 and A5 rated near the top of the scale (above 4) from all three viewpoints and A3 and A7 clearly lower (about 2.2-2.5), so the hue ranking did not depend on one camera angle. Differences from the original study: (1) only the viewpoint validation is rebuilt; the 21 photo-based hue and saturation images of Experiments 2-3 are not used because the published figure prints condition labels on them, and the Munsell chip task (Experiment 1), eye tracking and pupillometry are not included; (2) the paper says this phase used the same rating criteria but not its timing, so the 2 s grey screen and 8 s viewing follow the laboratory procedure of Experiment 2; (3) the paper does not print the question wording or scale labels, so the instruction text, question stems and anchors (1 = not at all, 5 = very much) are ours; (4) the age and gender questions are ours; the paper's laboratory participants were Chinese university students, here anyone can take part; (5) the renders are the figure copies (about 500 px wide).",
